\title{Completeness, Compactness and Exact Fibonacci Covering Combinatorics of the First-Disagreement Truth Space}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{30 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We study the space $\C=\{\zero,\one\}^{\mathbb N}$ of infinite binary \emph{truth streams} --- total assignments of yes/no answers to an enumerated sequence of queries --- equipped with the \emph{first-disagreement ultrametric} $d(x,y)=2^{-\fd(x,y)}$, in which the closed ball of radius $2^{-n}$ about a stream is exactly its depth-$n$ prefix class. We prove that $(\C,d)$ is totally bounded, with the explicit $2^{-n}$-net given by the $2^{n}$ depth-$n$ truncations; that it is complete, because Cauchy sequences stabilise coordinatewise and the coordinatewise limit is the metric limit; and hence that it is compact. We then analyse the golden-mean subshift $\G\subseteq\C$ of streams containing no two consecutive $\one$s, showing that it is closed (hence compact), invariant under the $2$-Lipschitz shift, and perfect, with explicit approximants. Finally we determine the covering combinatorics of $\G$ exactly at every dyadic scale: the depth-$n$ prefixes of $\G$ are precisely the length-$n$ words avoiding $\one\one$, of which there are $F_{n+2}$; the $F_{n+2}$ closed $2^{-n}$-balls centred at the zero-padded admissible words cover $\G$; and those centres are pairwise at distance strictly greater than $2^{-n}$. Covering number and packing number therefore coincide and equal $F_{n+2}$, whence the box dimension of $\G$ is $\log\varphi/\log 2=0.69424\ldots$ against $1$ for the ambient space. We contrast this metric deficit with a topological coincidence ($\G$ is homeomorphic to $\C$ by Brouwer's characterisation of the Cantor set) and a dynamical rigidity (the shifts have $1$ and $2$ fixed points respectively, hence are non-conjugate).
\end{abstract}

